  "title": "Y2K Color Palettes",
  "tagline": "Bubblegum pink, cyber lilac, sky blue — the early-2000s revival, historically grounded.",
  "description": "Y2K color is usually described as bubblegum and hot pink, icy and electric blue, translucent lilac, lime, and metallic silver. The surprise is how much of that sits in a color dictionary from 1933. The Wada plates at the top of this set land close to it: Eosine Pink (#f37f94) and Spinel Red (#f27291) for the pinks, Pale King's Blue (#a7d4e4) beside Blue (#006eb8) for icy against electric, Aconite Violet (#a36aa5) for the lilac end, Night Green (#87c540) for lime, and Neutral Gray (#b6bfc1) as the nearest thing to silver. The palettes after them were put together on this site from traditional Japanese color names, and they run softer: Nadeshiko (#EBA6B4) is a pale pink, Sora (#7AB2D3) a sky blue, Fuji (#B5A6C9) a pale wisteria. Two things no flat color can give you: chrome, which is a shine effect on a screen rather than a color, and the glow of a backlit display. Use these for nostalgia-driven packaging, streetwear capsules, beauty relaunches, and playful digital products.",
